<p>Lebanese and Israeli envoys are due to meet in Washington on Thursday for a second round of talks in two weeks, as Lebanon pushes to extend a fragile 10-day U.S.-mediated ceasefire set to expire on Sunday. The contacts stand out because they build on rare engagement between the two sides, with Beirut also working closely with France ahead of the meeting after Prime Minister Nawaf Salam met President Emmanuel Macron in Paris this week. The ceasefire remains shaky. Hezbollah said it fired rockets into northern Israel on Tuesday in response to what it called Israeli violations, while Israel accused Hezbollah of breaches as well. French officials said they support Lebanon&#x27;s territorial integrity and argued that Israeli troop withdrawal is a condition for stability, while Israeli officials said they are using diplomatic and military pressure to push for Hezbollah&#x27;s disarmament. Lebanon says it needs 500 million euros over the next six months to address the humanitarian fallout from six weeks of war that Lebanese authorities say killed more than 2,450 people and wounded more than 7,600.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/XRrBUMOQO4eJJSyQ4FgYVIwwnCyl8fgaukZK7hqFjODxttgWweHjqfTWkhG78ATvwn93He6000l30bO_grgbg1CwLxhAulusZdVUOG49j8Ly_2ju5xWTxGPEdk4w7ixeGyeqNzjQwtCLUstdVEPZAF_0ZJgCsxOMbgVG9WTfWqWBAkuPjNkGDXmuaYr996NGe8-PcDL_sQxR8AJfs_yCPmzIYF1PCEQ7ByNuVZwgbqK7_Qj8YU2Mzo8ijLjdsl7Ql9xJjkqH' alt='French President Emmanuel Macron and Lebanese Prime Minister Nawaf Salam upon his arrival for a meeting at the Elysée Palace in Paris, April 21, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>French backing: Macron said France would help Lebanese authorities prepare for negotiations with Israel even if Paris was not directly involved in the talks, calling France&#x27;s seat at the table a secondary issue compared with Lebanon&#x27;s interests.</li><li>Hezbollah stance: In a rare interview, senior Hezbollah official Wafiq Safa said the group would never give up all its weapons and tied any discussion to an Israeli withdrawal from Lebanon.</li><li>Diplomatic opening: Le Monde described Beirut&#x27;s outreach to Paris before the Washington meeting as an effort to break out of diplomatic isolation while facing pressure from Israel and the Trump administration.</li></ul><blockquote>Lebanon needs 500 million euros to tackle the humanitarian crisis over the next six months - Prime Minister Nawaf Salam</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Lebanese government: Prime Minister Nawaf Salam sought French help before the Washington meeting and said Lebanon needed outside support both for the negotiations and to meet urgent humanitarian needs after the fighting. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1993967/macron-vows-to-help-lebanon-prepare-for-talks-with-israel'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>French government: President Emmanuel Macron said France would help Lebanon prepare for the talks and affirmed that Israeli troop withdrawal from Lebanon is a precondition for stability. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1993967/macron-vows-to-help-lebanon-prepare-for-talks-with-israel'>Dawn</a>, <a href='https://www.timesofisrael.com/idf-says-hezbollah-breached-truce-launching-rockets-at-troops-drone-at-israel/'>Times of Israel</a>)</li><li>Israeli government: Israeli officials say they are using diplomatic and military pressure in an effort to disarm Hezbollah. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1993967/macron-vows-to-help-lebanon-prepare-for-talks-with-israel'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Hezbollah: Senior official Wafiq Safa rejected full disarmament and said Hezbollah ties its position to an end to what it describes as Israeli occupation in Lebanon. (<a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/show/in-rare-interview-top-hezbollah-leader-rejects-disarmament-calls-for-israeli-withdrawal'>PBS NewsHour</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/04/lebanon-hopes-extension-ceasefire-washington-meeting'>Lebanon hopes for extension of ceasef